- kb/reference/adr/040-scripts-directory-is-the-accumulation-substrate-for-ad-hoc-tooling.md — 040-`scripts/` is the accumulation substrate for ad hoc tooling — scripts/ is named the committed home for reusable ad hoc tooling, distinct from throwaway python3 heredocs and the installed package, with a cleanup norm and a promotion signal
- kb/notes/llm-code-boundaries-are-natural-checkpoints.md — LLM↔code boundaries are natural checkpoints — At each LLM↔code transition both semantic underspecification and execution indeterminism collapse simultaneously, making these boundaries natural places to anchor debugging, testing, and refactoring
- kb/notes/theory-and-methodology-form-a-two-layer-execution-system.md — Methodology with incomplete coverage and its live theory fallback form a two-layer execution system — In open or incompletely covered domains, the theory-derived fast path and live theory fallback co-execute while methodology-native content follows a separate maintenance regime
- kb/notes/session-history-should-not-be-the-default-next-context.md — Session history should not be the default next context — Storing execution history and loading it into the next agent call are separate decisions; chat and framework-owned tool loops conflate them by making session history the default next context
- kb/notes/storing-llm-outputs-is-constraining.md — Storing LLM outputs is constraining — Keeping a specific LLM output resolves semantic underspecification and freezes one run against execution indeterminism - a constraining move applied to artifacts
- kb/notes/skills-derive-from-methodology.md — Skills derive from methodology — The methodology→skill relationship is same-medium derivation, with the methodology retained as live fallback — distinct from codification and constraining
- kb/notes/skills-are-instructions-plus-routing-and-execution-policy.md — Skills are instructions plus routing and execution policy — Skills add structured discovery, user-facing invocation, and declarative execution policy (tool permissions, model override, context isolation) beyond the shared procedure
- kb/notes/charting-the-knowledge-access-problem-beyond-rag.md — Charting the knowledge-access problem beyond RAG — Brainstorming note that decomposes the "how should an agent find what it needs?" problem into storage substrate, pointers, navigation, synthesis, and maintenance beyond RAG-vs-filesystem debates
- kb/notes/llm-interpretation-errors-README.md — LLM interpretation errors — Three sources of deviation between intended and actual LLM output — prompt underspecification, execution indeterminism, and interpreter failure — plus oracle theory, error correction, and architectural responses for managing each
- kb/notes/agentic-systems-interpret-underspecified-instructions.md — Agentic systems interpret underspecified instructions — Separates semantic underspecification from execution indeterminism: natural-language specs admit multiple valid projections, while constraining commits one projection to precise code.
- kb/notes/traversal-improvements-should-be-deferred-via-logging-to-avoid-mid.md — Traversal improvements should be deferred via logging to avoid mid-task context switching — Loading writing methodology into an already-committed context window is expensive; a one-line log entry preserves the improvement signal at near-zero cost and lets a separate pass do the fix
- kb/notes/methodology-enforcement-is-constraining.md — Methodology enforcement is constraining — Instructions, skills, hooks, and scripts form a constraining gradient: later layers reduce underspecification and execution indeterminism; warning hooks fix the trigger but leave the response to an LLM, while blocking hooks and scripts fix both, so stable practices can compile into code
- kb/notes/scheduler-llm-separation-exploits-an-error-correction-asymmetry.md — Scheduler-LLM separation exploits an error-correction asymmetry — Bookkeeping and semantic operations have different error profiles across all three phenomena (underspecification, indeterminism, bias) — symbolic substrates eliminate all three for bookkeeping; mixing forces bookkeeping onto the expensive semantic-correction substrate
- kb/notes/semantic-sub-goals-that-exceed-one-context-window-become-scheduling.md — Semantic sub-goals that exceed one context window become scheduling problems — Some semantic subgoals exceed one context window, so they must be partitioned into smaller semantic judgments with symbolic collection, filtering, and staged summarization between them
- kb/notes/execution-indeterminism-is-a-property-of-the-sampling-process.md — Execution indeterminism is a property of the sampling process — The same prompt can produce different outputs across runs due to token sampling — this is a property of the execution engine, theoretically eliminable but practically ubiquitous, and often confused with the deeper issue of underspecification
- kb/notes/fix-what-the-executor-cant-determine-not-what-it-will.md — An author should fix what the executor can't determine, not what it will — Any artifact authored before execution — instruction, plan, spec, workshop frame, an orchestrator's task prompt — fixes details as authoring-time snapshots that go stale and consume the executor's search space; fix only the goal, constraints, what done means, and the genuinely arbitrary choices, and leave what the live situation determines to the executor
- kb/notes/underspecification-and-indeterminism-complicate-programming-for.md — Underspecification and indeterminism complicate programming for prompts in distinct ways — Indeterminism doubles test runs (statistical testing over distributions); underspecification doubles test targets (spec analysis for ambiguity). Conflating the two leads to misdiagnosis
- kb/notes/agent-memory-requirements/adaptation-survey-corroborates-memory-requirements.md — The adaptation survey corroborates memory requirements but misses artifact governance — The agentic-adaptation survey supports the memory requirements map by treating memory and skills as adaptive tools, but it needs substrate, form, lineage, and authority governance to become design guidance
- kb/notes/fixed-artifacts-split-into-exact-specs-and-proxy-theories.md — Fixed artifacts split into exact specs and proxy theories — Fixed artifacts are safe when their spec fully captures the problem; they are risky when they encode proxy theories whose components may not compose into the larger capability

